# §B. Required checks R1–R10, and the amendments

## B1. R6/R8 — the blocking finding: §3 requires the impossible

**v1.3-R0.1 §3 requires the canonical manifest to directly enumerate — with
byte count and SHA-256 — the "checksum, reconstruction, link-validation, and
independent-review receipts."**

**v1.2 §4 creates those artifacts after the manifest.** Order D: "checksums,
root receipt and validation summary generated **only from the verified manifest
and leaf set**." Order F: package-level independent review, later still.

A manifest cannot contain the digest of an artifact that does not yet exist, and
the artifacts in question cannot exist before it: `checksums.sha256` and
`root_hash_receipt.json` are *functions of* the manifest, and an
independent-review receipt is produced by a different line after the package is
assembled. This is not an ordering inconvenience that a careful implementer can
sequence around — it is a **circular dependency**, and for review receipts a hard
impossibility. Any hash tree's root commits to what existed when it was computed;
nothing created afterward can be inside it.

Consequence if adopted unamended: v0.4 is assembled against an unsatisfiable
clause, its manifest is judged incomplete under §3, and the capsule fails a
fourth time — with the failure looking like another implementation error rather
than a standards defect.

**Proposed replacement for §3 (exact text):**

> **3.1 PRE-ROOT LEAF SET (root-bearing).** The canonical manifest MUST directly
> enumerate every carrier that exists and has passed round-trip verification at
> manifest-generation time: the four primary human-readable capsule documents;
> every evidentiary source or frozen export required by the capsule
> specification; the exact frozen register-row carrier; native-object export
> identity records; and any reconstruction or link-validation receipt already
> published and verified before manifest generation. Each record MUST include
> artifact ID and title; Drive ID and permanent link; MIME type and declared
> export representation where applicable; byte count; SHA-256; evidence role;
> active/correction/superseded/failed/collision/provenance status; dependency
> relation; retrieval or revision identity. **The manifest MUST NOT enumerate
> itself.**
>
> **3.2 POST-ROOT ATTACHMENTS (root-referencing, non-root-bearing).** Artifacts
> that cannot exist before the manifest — `checksums.sha256`,
> `root_hash_receipt.json`, `validation_summary.json`, and independent-review
> receipts — MUST NOT be enumerated in the canonical manifest. Each MUST instead
> record the accepted ROOT_SHA256 and the canonical manifest Drive ID, binding it
> to the package **by reference to the root rather than by inclusion in it**.
>
> **3.3 DECLARED MEMBERSHIP.** The manifest MUST declare, in an explicit field,
> the exact membership of the §3.1 set **and the expected membership of the §3.2
> set**, so that omission in either class is detectable by a reviewer rather than
> invisible. Completeness of a capsule requires that every declared §3.2
> attachment exists and cites the accepted root; the root certifies only the
> §3.1 set.
>
> **3.4** Consolidated index carriers may be listed as additional §3.1 leaves but
> may not replace direct enumeration of any §3.1 member. A superseded, collision,
> failed, corrupt, or provenance-only object MUST either be excluded from the
> §3.1 set or listed with zero root-bearing evidence credit. Same-purpose
> alternate carriers may not be silently substituted.

§3.3 is what preserves the protection §3 was reaching for: without it, moving
receipts out of the root would let a package omit them silently. Declaring the
expected post-root set inside the rooted manifest keeps the completeness check
enforceable without the circularity.

## B2. R2 — the root object is non-circular, with one gap that must be closed

§2's construction is correct: ROOT_SHA256 over the exact stored raw bytes of one
declared Drive ID, after write-then-read verification, with parse/reserialize
demoted to a **validation control** that must reproduce stored bytes. That is the
right design, and the sentence "the bytes uploaded to Drive MUST already be
canonical" is the sentence that dissolves the whole A/B dispute: under a correct
implementation *stored bytes and canonical bytes are the same object*, so the two
root definitions coincide and any divergence between them is itself the defect.

**Gap: nothing prohibits self-reference.** §2 does not forbid the manifest from
containing ROOT_SHA256 or any digest of itself. An implementer who adds a
`root_sha256` convenience field creates precisely the circularity §2 was written
to prevent, and it would pass every check in §2 as drafted.

**Proposed addition to §2 (exact text):**

> The canonical manifest MUST NOT contain ROOT_SHA256, any digest of its own
> bytes, or any field derived from its own serialized content, and MUST NOT list
> itself among the enumerated leaves. ROOT_SHA256 is recorded only in post-root
> attachments under §3.2. A manifest containing a self-digest is noncanonical and
> receives zero root and sealing credit.

## B3. R3 — canonicalization is deterministic in intent but under-determined in text

§2's seven bullets are necessary and not sufficient. Four divergence channels
remain open, each capable of producing exactly the small byte deltas this program
has already seen:

1. **"Compact JSON serialization" is not a specification.** Separator choice
   (`,`/`:` versus `, `/`: `) is unstated; two conforming implementations differ
   by one byte per delimiter.
2. **Number formatting is unspecified** — `1` vs `1.0`, `1e3` vs `1000`, `-0`,
   float precision. This is the classic canonicalization trap and the standard is
   silent on it.
3. **String escaping is unspecified** — `\/` vs `/`, `\uXXXX` vs literal
   supplementary characters, hex case in escapes.
4. **Key-sort collation is unspecified** — "recursively sorted" by byte order,
   codepoint order, or UTF-16 code-unit order? These disagree on non-ASCII keys.

**Proposed replacement for §2's canonicalization bullet list (exact text):**

> JSON canonicalization SHALL follow **RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme)**,
> which fixes serialization, string escaping, number formatting, and UTF-16
> code-unit key ordering, with these capsule-specific addenda:
> (a) the JCS output is followed by **exactly one LF**, and any validator
> comparing against a stock JCS implementation MUST append that LF before
> comparison — JCS itself emits no trailing newline, and this one byte is
> otherwise a guaranteed false mismatch;
> (b) manifest values are **restricted to strings, integers exactly representable
> in IEEE-754 double, booleans, null, objects, and arrays; floating-point values
> are prohibited**, which removes number-formatting divergence by construction
> rather than by convention;
> (c) **every array's ordering rule MUST be stated inside the manifest** in an
> explicit field, not delegated to an external "capsule specification" that a
> reviewer may not hold;
> (d) UTF-8, no BOM, LF newlines throughout.

Addendum (a) is not pedantry. Candidate B's stored 9,409 versus claimed canonical
9,407 is a **two-byte** delta — exactly the magnitude produced by trailing-newline
and delimiter-level disagreements. I cannot determine its cause without the bytes
(§D), but a standard that leaves four one-to-two-byte divergence channels open
will keep manufacturing this class of dispute.

